<!--
     SPDX-FileCopyrightText: The LineageOS Project
     SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

     Device manifest (DEVICE_MANIFEST_FILE).

     Almost everything the vendor image declares arrives as one of the
     fragments under /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest/, which are shipped as blobs.
     What is left is:

     (a) the per-SoC part: the stock image carries manifest_kera.xml,
         manifest_sun.xml and manifest_tuna.xml side by side because Lenovo
         builds one vendor image for several SKUs. This device is TunaP
         (soc_id 694), so android.hardware.gatekeeper below is manifest_tuna.xml
         — the one HAL those fragments do not declare.

     (b) two HALs whose fragment files had to be dropped from
         proprietary-files.txt because the *tree* was winning the install race
         and declaring the wrong version. Details in each entry below.

     Why declaring them here rather than fighting over the fragment file:
     a fragment is only installed because something requests that output path.
     The blob line was the sole requester; the tree's copy-vintf-manifest-checked
     recipe (there are 174 of them, emitted unconditionally) then satisfied the
     target because kati keeps the LAST rule defined. Verified: power-default.xml,
     memtrack-default.xml, lights-default.xml and android.hardware.vibrator.xml
     all have that recipe and none of them is installed. Removing the blob line
     therefore removes the file entirely — and this manifest, which
     assemble_vintf merges into /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest.xml, is a
     deterministic place to put the declaration instead. It does not depend on
     kati rule ordering and it is reviewable in one file.
-->
<manifest version="8.0" type="device" target-level="202404">
    <hal format="aidl">
        <name>android.hardware.gatekeeper</name>
        <fqname>IGatekeeper/default</fqname>
    </hal>

    <!-- v5, not the v6 the tree's vendor/qcom/opensource/power/power.xml
         declared. The implementation we install is the stock blob
         /vendor/bin/hw/android.hardware.power-service, which is byte-identical
         to the factory binary and links android.hardware.power-V5-ndk.so.
         V6 is not installed at all (installed-files-vendor.txt has only V5).

         Independent confirmation: this device declares target-level 202404, and
         system/etc/vintf/compatibility_matrix.202404.xml lists
         android.hardware.power version "5" exactly. Declaring 6 claims a
         capability from a newer vendor API level than we ship.

         The AIDL version number carries no scheduling or battery policy — that
         lives in /vendor/etc/powerhint.xml plus the perf blobs
         (libqti-perfd-client.so), which we ship from stock either way. Moving to
         the tree's v6 service is a post-first-boot option, and would need care:
         that module installs its own config/sun/powerhint.xml, which would then
         collide with the stock one. -->
    <hal format="aidl">
        <name>android.hardware.power</name>
        <version>5</version>
        <fqname>IPower/default</fqname>
    </hal>

    <!-- v4, not the v5 that AOSP's
         hardware/interfaces/bluetooth/audio/aidl/default/bluetooth_audio.xml
         declared. This one is unambiguous: there is no
         android.hardware.bluetooth.audio-V5-ndk.so anywhere in the build — only
         V3 and V4 are installed — so v5 declares an interface version nothing
         in the image can serve. compatibility_matrix.202404.xml lists "3-4".
         Stock declares 4. -->
    <hal format="aidl">
        <name>android.hardware.bluetooth.audio</name>
        <version>4</version>
        <fqname>IBluetoothAudioProviderFactory/default</fqname>
    </hal>
</manifest>
